  "final_text": "Both pieces exist: the dashboard \"EFF-cdafda60 Web logs overview\" has five panels, and the rule \"EFF-cdafda60 5xx spike\" is enabled, runs every minute, and reports status `ok`.\n\n**Data exploration:** The data view has `timestamp` as its time field. The useful fields are `response` (status code, with a `response.keyword` sub-field for aggregation), `clientip`, `geo.dest`, `request`, `url`, `extension`, `bytes`, `agent`, and `machine.os`.\n\n**Dashboard panels** (time range last 1 year):\n1. Total requests (count metric)\n2. Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n3. Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n4. Requests by response code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n5. Top countries by requests (bar, terms on `geo.dest`, top 10)\n\n**Alert rule:** The `.es-query` rule runs every 1m over `kibana_sample_data_logs` with a 5-minute window and fires when the hit count is greater than 10. The query matches `response.keyword` against the wildcard `5??`, so it counts only three-digit 5xx codes.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- I tagged the rule `EFF-cdafda60`. I also used a 1-year time range on the dashboard, since the sample data's timestamps may not fall in the default last 7 days.\n- The rule's action list is empty, so it records alerts in the rule's history but does not notify anyone. Adding a connector would fix that.\n\nI did not verify the actual response-code values or whether the rule has fired. My shell's `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is empty, so I could only use the MCP tools, and I did not run a query against the data. The pie chart will show which codes are present. If the codes are stored differently than I assumed, the rule would match nothing.",
